# What never enters a Docker build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that bites. The obvious one is size: an
# installed `node_modules` here is well over a gigabyte, and it is uploaded to
# the daemon on every build whether a layer uses it or not.
#
# The one that actually causes bugs is *staleness*. `apps/iam-api/Dockerfile`
# copies the workspace in and builds it, so anything already built on the host
# would be copied over the top of a clean checkout — and Nx, seeing outputs that
# match its input hashes, would happily skip the work and ship a bundle compiled
# from a developer's laptop weeks ago. Excluding every build output makes the
# image a function of the source and nothing else.
#
# `.env` files are excluded for the third reason: this image must contain no
# customer-specific value and no secret (Doc 08 §5). Configuration arrives from
# the platform's secret store at run time.

# ── Dependencies and caches ────────────────────────────────────────────────
**/node_modules
.nx
**/.swc
**/.cache

# ── Build output (rebuilt inside the image) ────────────────────────────────
**/dist
**/out-tsc
**/.next
**/tsconfig.*.tsbuildinfo
apps/admin-web/public/manuals
docs/manuals/html

# ── Test artefacts ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
**/coverage
**/test-output
apps/iam-api-e2e/test-output

# ── Secrets and local configuration ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every `.env*`, at every depth — not just the root's. Session 41 turned this
# from tidiness into a correctness rule, because `apps/admin-web/Dockerfile`
# runs `next build` inside the context and Next **inlines** every
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` it finds in `apps/admin-web/.env`, `.env.production` or
# `.env.local` straight into the client bundle. A developer's local file naming
# `http://localhost:3000` would therefore produce a console image hard-wired to
# their laptop — with no warning at build time, and no symptom until a customer
# opens a console that cannot reach its own API. The default the image is
# supposed to carry is the same-origin `/api` of Doc 11 §8, and it only survives
# if nothing is here to override it.
#
# The examples are kept. They hold no value, they are the documentation an
# operator reads first, and `libs/config` has a test that keeps the root one
# honest.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# ── Repository and editor metadata ─────────────────────────────────────────
.git
.github
.gitignore
.vscode
.claude
Dockerfile
**/*.log

# ── Documentation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Excluded from the *image build* only. `docs/` is the authority for this system
# and is not optional anywhere else — it simply has no bearing on a compiled
# bundle, and Session 43 (manifests as release artifacts) is what will need a
# deliberate exception here.
docs
*.md
!README.md
